```text
FINANCE COMMITTEE
MINUTES
A meeting of the Finance Committee was held on Tuesday, August 5, 2025, in Council Chambers
and via electronic meeting, starting at 12:00 p.m. Those in attendance were as follows:
COMMITTEE MEMBERS:
Pete Layboum, Chairman; Mark Moody; Dr. Mark Rinne; and
Scott Roybal. Absent: Ken Esquibel.
AGENDA ITEMS
6.
APPLICATION/LICENSES/PERMITS:
a)
Transfer of ownership of restaurant liquor license application filed with the City
Clerk’s Office for Pothos Business LLC d/b/a Bella Fuoco, 2115 Warren Ave.,
Cheyenne, WY (license currently held by Penstemon Business LLC d/b/a Bella
Fuoco).
Mr. Roybal moved to approve, seconded by Mr. Moody. Motion carried by unanimous voice vote.
Kylie Soden, City Clerk, provided a staff report and Jim Weaver, commercial real estate broker
and applicant’s
representative,
was
available
for
questions.
Committee
member Roybal
commented on the item.
11.
ORDINANCE 3” READING
— Defining the terms and conditions of employment for
sworn officers of the Cheyenne Police Department for Fiscal Year 2025-2026.
Mr. Moody moved to approve on 3”’ and final reading, seconded by Dr. Rinne. Motion carried by
unanimous voice vote.
Captain David Janes, Police Department, provided a staff’ report.
14.
RESOLUTION
— Authorizing the City of Cheyenne to enter into a Memorandum of
Understanding with Cheyenne LEADS to establish and administer a Large Projects Gap
Finance Program, utilizing voter-approved
6thj Penny Sales and Use Tax Funds for
economic development. (SPONSORS
— MR. SEGRAVE AND MR. WHITE)
Dr. Rinne moved to adopt, seconded by Mr. Roybal. Motion carried by unanimous voice vote.
Mayor Patrick Collins, advised on challenges related to projects referred to in the 6th Penny Sales
and Use Tax ballot and the need to the proposed partnership. Betsey Hale, CEO Cheyenne LEADS.
provided information related to the proposed partnership. Chairman Laybourn and all committee
members commented on the item.
```

### Page 2 of 3

```text
15.
LEASES/CONTRACTS/LEGAL:
e)
Professional Services Agreement between the City of Cheyenne and TDSi- The
Design Studio, inc., for the high Plains Arboretum: Greenhouse. (State Parks and
Cultural Resources Funds)
Mr. Moody moved to accept the bid in an amount not to exceed $ 154.243.00, seconded by Dr.
Rinne. Motion carried by unanimous voice vote.
Jason Sanchez, Community Recreation & Events Director, provided a staff report and Randy
Byers, TDSi Inc., provided additional information. Chairman Laybourn and committee members
Rinne and Roybal commented on the item.
17.
CONSIDERATION OF BIDS/PURCHASES/REQUESTS FOR PROPOSALS:
b)
Consideration of Bid #S-28-25 for an Agreement between the City of Cheyenne
and Construction Specialists. Inc.. for the Ice Slicer Building
- Demolition and New
Steel Structure. (20 19-2022 Optional 1% Sales Tax Fund)
Dr. Rinne moved to accept the bid in an amount not to exceed $627,700.00, seconded by Mr.
Roybal. Motion carried by unanimous voice vote.
Adele Bartel, Public Works Staff Engineer, provided a staff report and Vicki Nemecek, Public
Works Director, provided information related to the funding of the proposed building. Chairman
Laybourn and committee member Moody commented on the item.
c)
Consideration of Bid #E-3-25 for two (2) Mid-Size Trucks for the City of
Cheyenne, Cheyenne Fire Rescue Department. (Public Safety Infrastructure Fees)
Mr. Moody moved to accept the bid from Ken Garff Cheyenne. in an amount not to exceed
$144,614.00, seconded by Mr. Roybal. Motion carried by unanimous voice vote.
TJ Barttelbort. Purchasing Manager, provided a staff report and Battalion Chief Neil Durante, Fire
Rescue Department, provided additional information. Committee member Rinne commented on
the item.
d)
Consideration of Bid #E-19-25 for furnishing Mid-Size SUVs and a Full-Size SUV
for the City of Cheyenne Compliance, Community Recreation and Events and
Cheyenne Fire Rescue Departments. (2023-2026 Optional 1% Sales Tax Fund and
One Time State Distribution Funds)
Mr. Moody moved to accept the bid from Fremont Motor Company, Lander, Wyoming. in an
amount not to exceed $274,676.00. seconded by Dr. Rinne. Motion carried by unanimous voice
vote.
```

